WEBVTT

00:01.684 --> 00:04.786
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.853 --> 00:08.306
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.390 --> 00:11.659
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.727 --> 00:14.962
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:15.014 --> 00:18.182
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.267 --> 00:21.619
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.687 --> 00:23.154
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.238 --> 00:25.073
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:25.140 --> 00:29.927
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:38.253 --> 00:41.656
Wow, congratulations on your
grand re-opening, Mort.

00:41.724 --> 00:43.641
Looks like your customers
are comin' back.

00:43.709 --> 00:46.794
Thanks, Lois. It's good
to be up and gouging again.

00:46.845 --> 00:48.713
Oh, careful.
Watch out there, Brian.

00:48.797 --> 00:49.981
Looks like the floor is wet.

00:50.049 --> 00:51.683
Excuse me.
I'm kind of in a hurry

00:51.750 --> 00:53.651
(screams)
Son of a bitch!

00:53.719 --> 00:55.253
Oh, my God!
Are you okay?

00:55.320 --> 00:57.772
I'm sorry. I've been
a little distracted lately.

00:57.840 --> 00:59.674
I think my wife has
been crossing the street

00:59.742 --> 01:00.875
with another man.

01:02.311 --> 01:04.145
God, I hope Alan
doesn't find out.

01:04.196 --> 01:05.747
Hey, forget that klutz.

01:08.000 --> 01:11.152
Hmm, maybe this one.

01:11.220 --> 01:13.488
Hey. Lookin' at you
pretty normal, huh?

01:13.555 --> 01:15.189
No!

01:15.257 --> 01:16.274
Aw, fudge.

01:16.341 --> 01:17.508
Wait, I know!

01:20.412 --> 01:21.646
I'll figure this out.

01:21.714 --> 01:23.514
Th-the pieces are all there.

01:23.582 --> 01:24.849
I-I'll go home
and figure this out.

01:26.902 --> 01:29.003
Aw, cool. Hey Mort,
what's this thing?

01:29.071 --> 01:31.606
Oh, that? That's my
"Come In! We're Open" sign.

01:31.673 --> 01:32.924
What's that,
like an iPad?

01:32.991 --> 01:34.409
No, it's a sign.

01:34.476 --> 01:35.893
Wish I had one of those.

01:35.961 --> 01:37.345
I've got a whole box
of them.

01:37.413 --> 01:39.147
Take it. It's yours.
No charge.

01:39.214 --> 01:41.349
Ugh, typical Jew.

01:45.320 --> 01:46.687
TV ANNOUNCER:
Coming this fall

01:46.755 --> 01:48.356
<i>from the producers
of Gone Baby Gone,</i>

01:48.424 --> 01:50.324
comes the riveting sequel:

01:50.392 --> 01:52.994
<i>Gone Severely Disabled
Baby Gone.</i>

01:53.061 --> 01:55.696
I promise you we are going
to find your daughter.

01:55.764 --> 01:57.999
Okay, but, you know,
do-do what you can,

01:58.066 --> 01:59.867
but seriously, don't,
like, kill yourself.

01:59.935 --> 02:02.570
Oh, and if you do find her,
honk this horn a couple times.

02:02.638 --> 02:04.806
It's the only way to get her
to stop biting you.

02:04.873 --> 02:06.474
But again, no rush.

02:06.542 --> 02:08.876
(knocking)

02:10.446 --> 02:11.929
Peter, what
are you doing?

02:11.997 --> 02:13.648
I'm just putting up
my new sign.

02:13.715 --> 02:15.016
Don't put that thing
on our door.

02:15.083 --> 02:16.167
If you want to hang it
somewhere,

02:16.235 --> 02:17.718
hang it in the basement.

02:17.786 --> 02:19.987
Oh, why? So you can hog it
while you're doing laundry?

02:20.055 --> 02:21.722
No way.
Peter, we're not a business.

02:21.790 --> 02:23.958
Hey, I saw your sign.
So, you're open?

02:24.026 --> 02:25.426
That's right.
What do you do?

02:25.494 --> 02:26.661
What do you need?
Milk.

02:26.728 --> 02:28.062
We have milk.

02:34.903 --> 02:36.304
Peter, I told you,
I don't like

02:36.371 --> 02:38.573
all these strangers
walking around in our house.

02:38.624 --> 02:41.442
They're not strangers, Lois;
they are our valued customers.

02:41.493 --> 02:43.744
And if we don't show them
we appreciate their loyalty,

02:43.812 --> 02:45.946
they're gonna go someplace else.
For what?!

02:45.998 --> 02:47.215
Not to alarm anyone,

02:47.282 --> 02:48.716
but some guy took me
in the closet

02:48.784 --> 02:49.984
and tried me on.

02:50.052 --> 02:52.520
She's right, Dad.
None of this makes any sense.

02:52.588 --> 02:53.888
Meg, go take your ten.

02:53.956 --> 02:55.623
And you may consider this
a verbal warning.

02:55.691 --> 02:57.391
Peter, I want
these people to leave.

02:57.459 --> 02:58.726
I fit,
by the way.

02:58.794 --> 03:00.261
Lois, I'd love nothing more

03:00.329 --> 03:01.429
than to sit here
and chat with you,

03:01.497 --> 03:03.030
but I have a business to run.

03:03.098 --> 03:04.682
Hi there.
May I help you folks?

03:04.750 --> 03:06.984
Yes, my family and I would like
our portrait painted.

03:07.052 --> 03:08.553
Well, you came
to the right place.

03:14.259 --> 03:15.827
Looking good.

03:15.894 --> 03:17.195
Almost done.

03:17.262 --> 03:19.197
And... there.

03:19.264 --> 03:20.448
Done.

03:22.434 --> 03:24.001
What have you done?!

03:24.069 --> 03:25.803
I painted the truth.

03:25.871 --> 03:27.505
I painted my truth.

03:31.543 --> 03:33.594
Gimme a spin, sweetie.

03:33.662 --> 03:34.879
What do you think, Mom?

03:34.947 --> 03:37.081
Is that the most beautiful
bride you've ever seen?

03:37.149 --> 03:38.182
I love it!

03:38.250 --> 03:39.617
Oh, it's just
so much money.

03:39.685 --> 03:41.335
It's true. It's true.

03:41.386 --> 03:42.453
Take the dress off,
sweetheart.

03:42.521 --> 03:43.955
Your mother says
you can't have it.

03:44.022 --> 03:45.223
W-wait, wait,
wait a minute.

03:45.290 --> 03:46.474
Do you really love it?

03:46.542 --> 03:49.026
I do. It just feels
like my wedding dress.

03:49.111 --> 03:51.462
Oh, Mom, she loves it.

03:51.530 --> 03:52.997
Okay.
We'll take it.

03:53.065 --> 03:56.200
Peter, what is that girl doing
in my wedding dress?!

03:56.285 --> 03:58.185
PETER: She gone?
No.

03:58.253 --> 03:59.387
Okay, well, while
I'm down here

03:59.454 --> 04:00.955
let me give you my card.

04:01.023 --> 04:02.490
There you go.

04:05.794 --> 04:08.930
Here you go. A half pound of
honey maple turkey, sliced thin.

04:08.997 --> 04:10.598
I wanted a
duplicate key made.

04:10.666 --> 04:12.032
You have a good one.

04:12.084 --> 04:13.317
Oh, hey, Joe.
What's going on?

04:13.385 --> 04:16.120
Peter, I'm afraid
I'm here to shut you down.

04:16.188 --> 04:17.955
You're running an
unlicensed business,

04:18.023 --> 04:19.290
and that's against the law.

04:19.358 --> 04:20.892
Aw, that is such bullcrap.

04:20.959 --> 04:22.643
Well, you can't fight
City Hall.

04:22.711 --> 04:24.478
Oh, we'll see about that!

04:27.182 --> 04:28.783
City Hall knows karate.

04:28.850 --> 04:31.352
I'm sorry, Peter, but the
government makes the rules.

04:31.420 --> 04:32.486
I just enforce them.

04:32.554 --> 04:33.754
I don't believe this, Joe.

04:33.822 --> 04:34.889
This is like
living in Russia.

04:34.957 --> 04:36.223
I might as well
just move there.

04:36.291 --> 04:37.625
You don't want to do that.

04:37.693 --> 04:39.594
It's dark; it's cold;
and hooking up

04:39.661 --> 04:42.063
is just a crapshoot
'cause of all the big coats.

04:42.130 --> 04:44.432
All right, drop
the coats on three.

04:44.499 --> 04:46.400
One... two...

04:46.468 --> 04:47.468
three!

04:47.536 --> 04:48.769
Yeah!
Oh...

04:52.741 --> 04:54.809
Hey, you ever
accidentally masturbate

04:54.876 --> 04:56.510
to young pictures
of your mom?

04:56.578 --> 04:58.613
Who the (bleep) starts
a conversation like that?

04:58.680 --> 05:00.181
I just sat down.

05:00.248 --> 05:01.616
Geez, what's
your problem, Peter?

05:01.683 --> 05:03.084
I'm sorry, Quagmire.

05:03.151 --> 05:05.086
I'm still just upset
about the damn government

05:05.153 --> 05:06.587
sticking its nose
in my business.

05:06.655 --> 05:07.822
I hear ya.

05:07.889 --> 05:10.358
Wasting our tax dollars
on ridiculous causes.

05:10.425 --> 05:11.943
We gotta protect
bald eagles?

05:12.010 --> 05:13.978
Bald eagles should be allowed
to make their own decisions!

05:16.148 --> 05:18.616
Honey, I don't care
that you're bald.

05:18.684 --> 05:20.217
I find you
just as attractive.

05:20.285 --> 05:21.602
It's not about you.

05:21.670 --> 05:24.839
It's about my confidence
as an eagle architect.

05:24.906 --> 05:26.273
<i>ANNOUNCER:
Just for Eagles will give you</i>

05:26.341 --> 05:28.442
<i>thicker, fuller,
more natural hair...</i>

05:28.493 --> 05:30.962
<i>so you can just focus
on eagle stuff.</i>

05:31.029 --> 05:33.230
You're an
excellent architect.

05:35.067 --> 05:36.500
Freakin' government.

05:36.568 --> 05:38.069
Giving all the
good hoses to firemen.

05:38.136 --> 05:39.437
I want them hoses.

05:39.504 --> 05:40.871
ANCHORMAN:
Good evening, I'm Tom Tucker.

05:40.939 --> 05:42.239
And I'm Joyce Kinney.

05:42.307 --> 05:43.808
Coming up: Autism:

05:43.875 --> 05:45.710
Is it real
or just another excuse

05:45.777 --> 05:47.778
for kids these days
to forget their manners?

05:47.846 --> 05:49.814
But first, thousands
are expected

05:49.881 --> 05:51.282
for tomorrow's
Tea Party rally

05:51.350 --> 05:53.250
celebrating their new
Quahog headquarters.

05:53.318 --> 05:54.485
That's right, Joyce.

05:54.553 --> 05:56.320
The Tea Party has gained
a sizable following

05:56.388 --> 05:58.155
based on their message
of low taxes,

05:58.223 --> 06:00.391
limited government,
and happy birthday, Tom...

06:00.459 --> 06:03.027
Oh, my God, you guys!

06:03.095 --> 06:04.395
Tea Party, huh?

06:04.463 --> 06:06.597
Well, I'd like to be
part of a movement,

06:06.665 --> 06:09.066
but what movement would want
an angry fat guy

06:09.134 --> 06:10.968
with a lot of opinions
who hates listening?

06:11.036 --> 06:13.070
For more details
on the Tea Party platform,

06:13.138 --> 06:16.007
we now go to this
totally boss fireworks show.

06:16.091 --> 06:19.927
♪ ♪

06:23.648 --> 06:25.282
Oh, my God! That's everything
I believe in!

06:25.350 --> 06:27.758
Why are they trying
to take that away from us?

06:27.783 --> 06:28.319
I hear ya.

06:28.320 --> 06:29.470
Well, that's it.

06:29.538 --> 06:31.238
I am going down
to that rally tomorrow

06:31.306 --> 06:32.807
and I am joining
the Tea Party!

06:32.874 --> 06:35.476
Oh, wait, that's the same time
as my men's knitting club,

06:35.544 --> 06:37.545
although they have been
really critical lately.

06:40.282 --> 06:42.383
You're still not knitting
honestly, Peter.

06:42.451 --> 06:44.218
You're knitting like
you want us to like you.

06:44.286 --> 06:45.686
It's not coming from inside.

06:45.754 --> 06:47.388
It's very superficial.

06:47.456 --> 06:49.657
Thank you!
You've made my decision easier!

06:49.725 --> 06:51.425
Yes! That! Knit that!

06:58.345 --> 07:00.346
Good Tea Party morning,
everyone!

07:00.413 --> 07:01.847
Peter, you joined
the Tea Party?!

07:01.915 --> 07:03.048
That's right, Brian.

07:03.116 --> 07:04.483
I finally got something
better to do

07:04.551 --> 07:06.118
with my Saturdays
than sit at the mall

07:06.186 --> 07:09.088
and watch Japanese girls
laugh at normal conversation.

07:09.155 --> 07:11.290
So, I drove to work
this morning...

07:11.358 --> 07:13.025
(all giggling)

07:13.093 --> 07:14.493
And I had to stop
for coffee.

07:14.561 --> 07:16.261
(all giggling)

07:16.329 --> 07:18.397
So, there's a Starbucks
near my house.

07:18.465 --> 07:21.767
(all laughing)

07:21.835 --> 07:23.435
You people
are a circus.

07:23.503 --> 07:25.487
Finally, I'm part of
a movement of regular people

07:25.555 --> 07:27.322
trying to take back
our government.

07:27.390 --> 07:28.624
Look, the Tea Party isn't

07:28.692 --> 07:30.459
the grassroots movement
you think it is.

07:30.527 --> 07:31.927
It's actually funded
by Big Business types

07:31.995 --> 07:34.430
who are using you to get
the government out of their way

07:34.497 --> 07:35.964
so they can roll over
everyone.

07:36.032 --> 07:38.634
Mom held hands with a woman
at the gas station this morning.

07:38.702 --> 07:41.403
Don't know what that means.
Just reporting it.

07:41.471 --> 07:42.905
All I'm saying is
you're being used

07:42.972 --> 07:44.306
and you're too clueless
to know it.

07:44.391 --> 07:46.892
Boy, you just think
you're so superior, don't you?

07:46.960 --> 07:49.428
Like that first creature
to walk on dry land.

07:50.814 --> 07:52.281
Hey, where'd you go?

07:52.348 --> 07:54.233
Ah, I went for a jog.
What's a jog?

07:54.300 --> 07:56.819
It's a great way to stay
in shape is what it is.

07:56.903 --> 07:58.320
Is it like a swim?

07:58.405 --> 07:59.805
(laughing condescendingly)

07:59.873 --> 08:01.940
No, no, it's, it's
nothing like a swim.

08:05.412 --> 08:07.596
Wow, a lot of people here today.

08:07.664 --> 08:09.865
Yeah. Man,
I love street fairs.

08:09.933 --> 08:11.200
They got rides, games,

08:11.284 --> 08:14.052
and ethnic food cooked horribly
by white Americans.

08:14.120 --> 08:16.305
Tack-os!
Hot tack-os here!

08:16.372 --> 08:17.923
Oh, are those tack-os?

08:17.991 --> 08:19.858
You better believe
they're tack-os.

08:19.926 --> 08:21.493
I love tack-os!

08:21.561 --> 08:23.195
Especially on a
corn tor-tilla.

08:23.263 --> 08:27.065
Oh! I love tack-os
on a corn tor-tilla!

08:29.569 --> 08:31.570
Boy, they're really
against socialism.

08:31.638 --> 08:34.206
That's right. The Tea Party
is all about self-reliance.

08:34.274 --> 08:35.991
Look, there's even
a Tunnel of Self-Love.

08:44.484 --> 08:47.085
Would you like to buy a photo
of yourself on the ride, sir?

08:47.153 --> 08:48.187
No, I would not.

08:48.254 --> 08:49.655
Welcome, everyone!

08:49.722 --> 08:52.691
It's great to see
so many regular people out here.

08:52.759 --> 08:54.793
Folks who are tired
of big government

08:54.861 --> 08:56.728
and are ready to stand up
for their rights.

08:56.796 --> 08:58.630
Yeah!
Down with the Spend-o-crats!

08:58.698 --> 08:59.698
PETER:
We are Marshall!

08:59.766 --> 09:01.667
And now, it's my privilege

09:01.734 --> 09:04.770
to introduce another regular,
blue-collar guy,

09:04.838 --> 09:08.674
our Quahog chapter spokesman:
Joe Workingman!

09:08.741 --> 09:09.992
(cheering and applause)

09:12.145 --> 09:15.397
Good afternoon, friends
and socioeconomic equals!

09:15.465 --> 09:17.633
Is anyone else out there
sick of government crap?

09:17.700 --> 09:19.268
Oh, my God.

09:19.352 --> 09:21.670
It's like he's saying everything
I'm listening to.

09:21.738 --> 09:23.038
That's right.

09:23.106 --> 09:25.174
The government wants to tell you
what foods to eat.

09:25.241 --> 09:26.658
(booing)

09:26.726 --> 09:28.293
And what church
you can go to.

09:28.361 --> 09:29.945
(booing)

09:30.029 --> 09:31.897
And that you can't
own a chimpanzee

09:31.965 --> 09:33.265
because you're not
responsible enough.

09:33.333 --> 09:34.850
(booing)

09:34.918 --> 09:36.084
I would feed it!

09:36.152 --> 09:37.986
Also, the government
wants to tell you

09:38.054 --> 09:39.488
how many children
you can have.

09:39.556 --> 09:41.456
What?! No!

09:41.524 --> 09:42.958
And the government
wants to tell you

09:43.026 --> 09:45.027
you can't throw
your old TVs into the river.

09:45.094 --> 09:47.763
Then how I supposed
to find TV?

09:47.831 --> 09:50.999
If you join the Tea Party,
together, we can fix all that.

09:51.067 --> 09:53.135
But you probably don't wanna
join the Tea Party

09:53.203 --> 09:58.357
because all you get are these
stupid, awesome keychains!

09:58.424 --> 10:01.326
Oh, my God, it's the keychain
from the dream!

10:04.781 --> 10:07.015
I got to say that guy
made a lot of sense.

10:07.083 --> 10:09.251
He sure did. The government's
really gotten outta hand.

10:09.319 --> 10:11.453
Yeah, those bastards
have ruined everything.

10:11.521 --> 10:13.856
The same way the Village People
ruined any gathering

10:13.923 --> 10:16.258
of a cop, an Indian chief,
and a construction worker.

10:16.326 --> 10:19.061
Oh, my God, can we get
a picture with you guys?

10:19.128 --> 10:20.395
We're not those guys.

10:20.463 --> 10:21.830
Yes, please
leave us alone.

10:21.898 --> 10:23.765
We're very busy.

10:23.833 --> 10:25.534
Now, where were we?
Oh, yeah.

10:25.602 --> 10:27.536
We've gotta solve
that security problem

10:27.604 --> 10:29.805
for the Indian Casino
we're constructing.

10:29.873 --> 10:31.206
Hey, you guys want
to head to the Clam?

10:31.274 --> 10:33.191
I'll catch up with you.
I got to hit the john.

10:38.648 --> 10:40.732
Hey, buddy. Is there
a bathroom around here,

10:40.800 --> 10:42.150
or a second floor window?

10:43.369 --> 10:44.570
(gasps)
Carter?!

10:44.637 --> 10:46.088
Oh... hey.

10:46.155 --> 10:47.656
You're involved
with the Tea Party?

10:47.724 --> 10:48.924
Wait a minute.

10:48.992 --> 10:50.559
This is what Brian
was warning me about:

10:50.627 --> 10:52.327
that the Tea Party
is run by rich guys

10:52.395 --> 10:53.695
who are only out for themselves.

10:53.763 --> 10:55.047
Brian?

10:55.098 --> 10:57.199
You mean that arrogant,
unemployed dog of yours?

10:57.267 --> 10:59.268
Peter, he's just one
of those liberal elites

10:59.335 --> 11:01.003
who spreads lies
and hates patriots.

11:01.070 --> 11:02.704
Yeah, he kinda is.

11:02.772 --> 11:05.707
You and I are both businessmen
who work hard and pay taxes.

11:05.775 --> 11:07.809
Except that I make
10,000 times what you make.

11:07.877 --> 11:09.745
Yeah, we're the same,
you and me.

11:09.812 --> 11:11.113
You know, Peter, the truth is,

11:11.180 --> 11:12.948
I could actually use
a regular guy like you

11:13.016 --> 11:14.283
to help get our message out,

11:14.350 --> 11:17.486
and I know
Joe Workingman agrees with me.

11:17.553 --> 11:19.087
Love to have you
on board, Peter.

11:19.155 --> 11:21.940
Wow, thanks, Joe.
You can count on me.

11:22.008 --> 11:23.825
Aw, Carter, you just missed
Joe Workingman.

11:23.893 --> 11:25.060
(laughs)

11:25.128 --> 11:27.162
Oh, I think he'll
know I was here.

11:27.230 --> 11:30.181
(laughing)

11:30.233 --> 11:31.533
(laughing maniacally)

11:31.601 --> 11:34.152
(chuckling nervously)

11:37.473 --> 11:39.274
I'm not sure
what's happening.

11:41.544 --> 11:44.146
Oh, hi, Peter.
How was your rally?

11:44.213 --> 11:45.781
Oh, it was great,
and now I know

11:45.848 --> 11:47.215
it's the right cause
to get behind

11:47.283 --> 11:49.551
'cause a smart guy
like your dad is involved.

11:49.619 --> 11:51.420
What, Carter was
at the Tea Party rally?

11:51.487 --> 11:52.821
Yeah, he was running
the whole thing.

11:52.889 --> 11:54.690
Peter, what did I tell you
about rich guys?

11:54.757 --> 11:56.491
Look, I know what
you're thinking, Brian,

11:56.559 --> 11:57.626
but don't worry;
it's all okay.

11:57.694 --> 11:58.994
Turns out
you're the enemy.

11:59.062 --> 12:00.462
They're the ones
who are on our side.

12:00.530 --> 12:02.030
Oh, of course,
Carter Pewterschmidt,

12:02.098 --> 12:04.533
an industrialist with
oil refineries in this city,

12:04.600 --> 12:05.934
is on your side.

12:06.002 --> 12:07.469
He wants to get rid
of environmental regulations

12:07.537 --> 12:08.637
for you.

12:08.705 --> 12:09.972
Brian, he doesn't
get sarcasm.

12:10.039 --> 12:12.074
No, it's not just environmental
regulations, Ban.

12:12.141 --> 12:13.208
Carter wants
to get rid

12:13.276 --> 12:15.344
of the whole city government
of Quahog.

12:15.411 --> 12:18.046
He even put me in charge
of the PR campaign to do it.

12:18.114 --> 12:19.581
Get rid of the government?

12:19.649 --> 12:22.384
Peter, if I know my dad,
he's probably using you.

12:22.452 --> 12:24.119
Lois, I'm so glad
you're done with the dishes

12:24.187 --> 12:25.988
so that you can hang out
and chime in on this.

12:26.055 --> 12:27.990
Actually, Peter, I
haven't done the dishes.

12:28.057 --> 12:29.291
Oh, you haven't?

12:29.359 --> 12:30.692
Well, then,
I'm confused.

12:30.760 --> 12:32.561
Oh, he does get sarcasm.

12:35.865 --> 12:37.032
All right,
now, remember,

12:37.100 --> 12:38.767
the Tea Party is a
grassroots movement.

12:38.835 --> 12:40.836
We're not covered by
the lame-stream media,

12:40.903 --> 12:42.971
so we got to reach out to
the common man individually.

12:43.039 --> 12:46.091
Right, common man.

12:46.142 --> 12:47.676
WOMAN:
Hello.

12:47.744 --> 12:50.762
Ah, geez, it's... it's a girl.

12:50.813 --> 12:52.614
May I speak to the man
of the house?

12:52.699 --> 12:55.834
This is the woman of the house,
and there is no man living here.

12:55.902 --> 12:57.369
No?

12:57.436 --> 13:00.188
W-W-What is... I-I... how are
you paying for this phone?

13:00.256 --> 13:02.057
I'm a lawyer.

13:02.125 --> 13:04.292
Oh, I see, and are you
in your little courthouse

13:04.360 --> 13:05.861
right now
representing Barbie?

13:05.928 --> 13:07.229
(click, dial tone)

13:07.296 --> 13:09.314
Okay, one vote
for the Tea Party.

13:09.382 --> 13:11.116
All right, kiss
government good-bye.

13:11.184 --> 13:13.785
Yeah, I can't wait to fire
all them city employees,

13:13.853 --> 13:16.254
starting with that half-asleep
construction worker.

13:23.079 --> 13:24.329
What the hell?

13:24.397 --> 13:25.864
W-W-Which one is it--
stop or go forward?

13:25.932 --> 13:26.848
This is a big deal.

13:26.933 --> 13:27.833
They're not at all similar.

13:30.620 --> 13:32.037
Ah, no, no, no, no.

13:32.105 --> 13:33.939
You, sir, have lost my business.

13:36.509 --> 13:39.111
("Yankee Doodle Dandy" playing)

13:58.014 --> 14:02.050
(crowd jeering)

14:02.118 --> 14:04.569
People, please,
first things first.

14:04.637 --> 14:07.672
Is there anyone here
for the couch off of Craigslist?

14:07.740 --> 14:08.507
I am.

14:08.574 --> 14:10.408
Oh, good, come on in.

14:13.980 --> 14:16.481
<i>That is the picture
from Craigslist.</i>

14:16.549 --> 14:17.482
No way it is.

14:17.550 --> 14:18.483
It is.

14:18.551 --> 14:21.119
No way it is.

14:21.187 --> 14:23.038
All right, what do
the rest of you want?

14:23.106 --> 14:25.173
We want the city government
shut down.

14:25.241 --> 14:27.192
(cheering)

14:27.260 --> 14:28.610
Oh, I see.

14:28.678 --> 14:31.146
And all of you feel
the same way?

14:31.214 --> 14:32.347
ALL: Yes!
MAN: No!

14:32.415 --> 14:34.116
(gunshot)
ALL: Yes!

14:34.183 --> 14:36.718
Well, I'm nothing
if not a public servant,

14:36.786 --> 14:38.920
and the public has made
its feelings known.

14:38.988 --> 14:43.358
I hereby declare Quahog's
city government disbanded.

14:43.426 --> 14:45.610
(cheering)

14:45.678 --> 14:50.415
It has been my pleasure to serve
you, and now I take my leave.

14:51.818 --> 14:53.718
This column is mine.

14:53.786 --> 14:56.121
I brought it from home.

15:01.961 --> 15:03.462
Good afternoon, I'm Tom Tucker.

15:03.529 --> 15:06.565
Our top story: Quahog is now
completely government-free,

15:06.632 --> 15:09.868
which means I'm free
of all broadcast regulations,

15:09.936 --> 15:12.237
so no more trips to the bathroom
for this reporter,

15:12.305 --> 15:14.639
just a big-ass coffee can
under the desk.

15:19.812 --> 15:21.363
Hello, family.

15:21.431 --> 15:23.332
Peter, what the hell
were you doing out there?

15:23.399 --> 15:25.734
You know, for years I've been
sick of taking the roads home,

15:25.802 --> 15:27.302
so today I took the yards.

15:27.370 --> 15:28.603
Didn't hit one light.

15:28.671 --> 15:30.072
Man, getting rid
of the government has been

15:30.139 --> 15:32.374
the best thing to happen
around here in a long time.

15:32.442 --> 15:33.959
That's right.

15:34.027 --> 15:36.028
Without government I'm free
to take a lot of mescaline

15:36.095 --> 15:38.030
and drive to Vegas.

15:41.300 --> 15:43.985
Wait till you see
Debbie Reynolds on stage.

15:44.053 --> 15:45.771
Yeah, and now
my Spanish teacher and I

15:45.838 --> 15:47.906
can finally hold hands
in the halls.

15:47.974 --> 15:50.525
<i>┬┐Meg, d├│nde est├б
la biblioteca?</i>

15:50.610 --> 15:53.345
<i>Because I'm going to bang you
in the biblioteca.</i>

15:53.413 --> 15:55.080
Well, I'm glad you're all
enjoying your freedom,

15:55.147 --> 15:56.948
but have you taken a look
out the window lately?

15:59.502 --> 16:00.602
What is that?

16:00.670 --> 16:02.237
Where's all that
smoke coming from?

16:02.305 --> 16:04.406
The Pewterschmidt Industries
refinery,

16:04.474 --> 16:07.109
and it's not smoke;
it's a giant cloud of pollution.

16:07.176 --> 16:08.660
Oh, my God.

16:08.711 --> 16:11.396
If that gets past the bad
neighborhoods and reaches us,

16:11.464 --> 16:12.997
that's going to be
a big problem.

16:13.049 --> 16:13.999
They can't do that.

16:14.067 --> 16:15.167
We should report them.

16:15.234 --> 16:16.518
To who?
There's no government.

16:16.586 --> 16:19.471
Then it's up to us, the
people, to find a solution.

16:19.539 --> 16:21.423
Close the curtains.

16:21.491 --> 16:22.974
Ah, Tea Party.

16:23.042 --> 16:25.577
Damn it, this is what I was
afraid was going to happen.

16:25.645 --> 16:27.612
This town is going
to go to hell.

16:27.680 --> 16:29.347
I now pronounce you
husband and wife.

16:29.415 --> 16:31.199
You may kiss
the bride.

16:32.835 --> 16:34.703
I'm glad we waited.

16:36.239 --> 16:38.206
(bleating)

16:40.243 --> 16:42.294
Giraffity.

16:42.361 --> 16:43.895
Yeah, see,
that's not mine.

16:43.963 --> 16:46.231
Hey, Mort, what gives
with the blond hair?

16:46.299 --> 16:48.066
Yeah, you look
weird like that.

16:48.134 --> 16:50.969
Historically, in instances
of mass lawlessness,

16:51.037 --> 16:54.423
the first thing they do
is round up the Jews.

16:54.490 --> 16:57.209
Excuse me, fellow Aryan,
have you seen any Jews around?

16:57.276 --> 16:58.477
Is there a reward?

16:58.544 --> 17:00.645
Wait a minute.
Are you Jewish?

17:00.713 --> 17:03.815
I mean, uh,
unashamed locker room nudity.

17:03.883 --> 17:05.150
This guy's kosher.

17:05.218 --> 17:06.151
Oops.

17:06.219 --> 17:07.385
Let's get him!

17:11.457 --> 17:12.824
Good afternoon,
I'm Tom Tucker.

17:12.892 --> 17:15.627
It's day five of the new
government-less Quahog,

17:15.695 --> 17:17.529
and I'm wearing
a T-shirt.

17:17.597 --> 17:19.531
Tom, I don't think
the government

17:19.599 --> 17:20.966
was requiring you
to wear a suit.

17:21.033 --> 17:23.468
Well, they were stopping me
from punching you in the face.

17:23.536 --> 17:24.669
Is that what you want?

17:24.737 --> 17:25.904
No, you know what?
You know what?

17:25.955 --> 17:27.122
Give me your wallet.
What?

17:27.190 --> 17:28.924
You heard me,
I said give me your wallet.

17:30.476 --> 17:31.743
This is a crappy wallet.

17:31.811 --> 17:33.912
You have bad stuff.

17:33.980 --> 17:34.963
Oh, what the hell?

17:35.031 --> 17:37.115
Lois, the TV
went off again.

17:37.183 --> 17:38.717
It's the
electricity, Peter.

17:38.784 --> 17:41.419
There are power lines down,
and there's no one to fix 'em.

17:41.487 --> 17:43.488
The cops don't come,
the toilets don't flush,

17:43.556 --> 17:45.957
and there's garbage
piling up in the streets.

17:46.025 --> 17:48.460
Ryan Phillippe's been
out there for days.

17:48.528 --> 17:50.629
Would anyone like
to be impregnated?

17:50.696 --> 17:52.280
We had a nice town, Peter.

17:52.348 --> 17:53.915
I had a nice life,

17:53.983 --> 17:57.869
but you and your antigovernment
buddies ruined it.

17:57.937 --> 17:59.988
Hey, so how's that
Tea Party going, huh?

18:00.056 --> 18:02.357
More like
T.P. Party.

18:02.425 --> 18:04.459
Toilet paper.

18:04.527 --> 18:05.861
I don't know
what happened, Brian.

18:05.928 --> 18:07.896
Not having a government
worked great in Somalia,

18:07.964 --> 18:09.865
but somehow we seem
to have botched it all up.

18:09.932 --> 18:11.600
Looks that way.

18:11.667 --> 18:13.298
Wait a minute. There might be
a way out of this.

18:13.323 --> 18:14.019
What?

18:14.020 --> 18:15.654
We just talk to the guy
who got us into this.

18:15.721 --> 18:16.922
He'll know what to do.

18:29.752 --> 18:32.020
Mr. Pewterschmidt.
Mr. Pewterschmidt.

18:32.088 --> 18:33.355
CARTER (over intercom):
Go away.

18:33.422 --> 18:35.724
But, Mr. Pewterschmidt,
you got to do something.

18:35.791 --> 18:37.692
The potholes ain't
getting fixed no more.

18:37.760 --> 18:39.594
The stoplights ain't working.

18:39.662 --> 18:42.297
Tampon commercials
no longer use blue liquids

18:42.365 --> 18:43.765
in their demonstrations.

18:43.833 --> 18:45.934
(all groan)

18:46.002 --> 18:47.969
Make it blue.
That's always been the deal!

18:48.037 --> 18:50.505
You show whatever you want,
but you make it blue!

18:50.573 --> 18:51.673
Come on.
You got to help us out.

18:51.741 --> 18:52.874
Go to hell.

18:52.942 --> 18:54.342
I don't think he's going
to help, Peter.

18:54.410 --> 18:56.711
But if rich people aren't
looking out for us, who is?

18:56.779 --> 18:58.680
Well, maybe we need
to look out for each other.

18:58.748 --> 19:01.583
Come on, Brian.
It's time to make this right.

19:01.651 --> 19:05.136
Wait, before you go,
take two steps to the left.

19:05.187 --> 19:06.121
Look up.

19:06.188 --> 19:08.373
Okay, smile.

19:08.441 --> 19:09.841
Aw, I suck.

19:09.892 --> 19:11.977
(indistinct shouting)

19:18.985 --> 19:21.937
People of Quahog,
I have something to say.

19:22.004 --> 19:23.538
Now that we've
freed ourselves

19:23.606 --> 19:25.407
from the terrible
shackles of government,

19:25.474 --> 19:27.742
it's time to replace it
with something better.

19:27.810 --> 19:30.412
(all grumbling)

19:30.479 --> 19:32.914
The first thing we need
is a system of rules

19:32.982 --> 19:34.583
that everyone
must live by.

19:34.667 --> 19:36.368
(all grumbling)

19:36.435 --> 19:37.802
Got to have rules.

19:37.870 --> 19:39.738
And since we can't spend
all our time making rules,

19:39.805 --> 19:42.641
I think that we should elect
some people to represent us,

19:42.708 --> 19:45.527
and they should make rules
and choices on our behalf.

19:45.595 --> 19:46.728
(all murmuring in agreement)

19:46.812 --> 19:49.547
That's probably a good idea.

19:49.615 --> 19:52.300
Now, this may be kind of
expensive, so I got a plan:

19:52.368 --> 19:53.768
everyone should have
to give some money

19:53.836 --> 19:55.604
from their salaries
each year.

19:55.671 --> 19:57.472
Poor people will give
a little bit of money

19:57.540 --> 19:59.474
and rich people will give
a larger amount of money,

19:59.542 --> 20:01.910
and our representatives
will use all that money

20:01.978 --> 20:03.912
to hire some people
who will then provide us

20:03.980 --> 20:05.981
with social order
and basic services.

20:06.048 --> 20:07.532
(cheering and applause)

20:09.952 --> 20:13.254
Ugh, there's not one shirt
I wish I had out there.

20:13.322 --> 20:14.756
Now, it won't be perfect.

20:14.824 --> 20:17.508
Some of our representatives
may end up being bastards.

20:17.560 --> 20:19.144
But you know what?
That's okay

20:19.195 --> 20:20.812
'cause later we're going to have
more elections,

20:20.863 --> 20:23.365
and we can use those elections
to get rid of the bad guys

20:23.449 --> 20:24.783
and replace 'em with good guys,

20:24.850 --> 20:26.935
and then the system
will just keep going on and on

20:27.003 --> 20:28.036
just like that.

20:28.038 --> 20:29.270
(cheering and applause)

20:29.355 --> 20:30.388
So who's with me?

20:30.456 --> 20:33.358
(cheering)

20:33.426 --> 20:36.077
Will you join me
in trying this new crazy thing?

20:36.145 --> 20:37.996
(cheering)

20:40.198 --> 20:42.149
Then let's do it.

20:42.217 --> 20:45.135
(cheering)

20:45.203 --> 20:47.364
Yeah, and we did it all
without government.

20:47.933 --> 20:49.183
I'm very proud
of you, Peter.

20:49.853 --> 20:52.187
Oh, Lois, that means
nothing to me.

20:52.310 --> 20:54.811
I only care what they say on
the Internet message boards.

20:54.812 --> 21:55.812
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru